.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-open - Open the admin app in the browser, on a payment's page when given its id


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 open [rail0Id|uuid] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Open the rail0 admin app in the browser: its home page, or — given a payment's
rail0_id (0x…) or UUID — that payment's page.

.PP
--console opens the operator console's page for the payment instead of the merchant's
(it needs an admin session in the browser). --print only prints the URL, as it does
when there is no terminal or with --agent, so a script can pass it on.

.PP
The admin's address is the admin-url setting (RAIL0_ADMIN_URL), or else the one the
current environment declares: development and staging have one, production does not
— there, set it with 'rail0 config set admin-url https://…'.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--console\fP[=false]
	Open the operator console's page for the payment (admin session)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for open

.PP
\fB--print\fP[=false]
	Only print the URL; do not open a browser


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 open                          # the admin's home page
  rail0 open 0xabc123…                # that payment's page
  rail0 open 0xabc123… --console      # the console's page for it (operators)
  rail0 open 0xabc123… --print        # just the URL
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0(1)\fP
